  }, "The Myth of Sisyphus"), ", which I\u2019d been reading throughout the trip. Through the story of Sisyphus\u2014a figure from myth condemned to push a boulder up a hill endlessly\u2014the book thinks through the idea that human life is fundamentally absurd. It\u2019s not a guidebook about how to live happily, and it\u2019s not a set of moral instructions saying \u201Cthis is the right way.\u201D Camus doesn\u2019t demand that readers live like the book. And by the book\u2019s own logic, this book itself doesn\u2019t have much meaning in anyone\u2019s life, either."), mdx("p", null, "While reading, I realized I hadn\u2019t started it because I thought \u201Cliving in revolt is cool and right.\u201D Instead, I think I was reading with an impudent question: whether it\u2019s even possible to keep that attitude of revolt while living an everyday life\u2014something like, \u201CFine. Let\u2019s see if you can actually live well like that.\u201D Now I think I\u2019ll just reflect on the existential task that remains: living in an absurd world, and what it means to \u201Crevolt\u201D inside it."), mdx("p", null, "I used up all the blister-prevention tape I bought when I started the trek. It reduces friction on the sole so it helps prevent blisters in the first place, but it\u2019s also incredibly useful because even after a blister forms, you can tape over it and keep walking. If I had to pick \u201Cmust-have\u201D items from this trek, I\u2019d say: blister-prevention tape, the Garmin 570 watch (for hiking route features), the NAROO Mask all-season outdoor mask, and the Patagonia Torrentshell 3L jacket (honestly the best windbreaker I\u2019ve ever owned)."), mdx("h1", {
